\'97\'97\'97\'97\'97\'97\'97\'97\'97\'97\'97\'97\'97\'97\'97\'97\'97\'97\'97\'97\
\'93They look 
\f2\i ridiculous
\f1\i0 ,\'93 said Kathy. \'94Nobody looks like that. I\'92ve been a doctor for a long time and I have never once walked into a room and thought, hello, everyone here is at the absolute top of their form. It doesn\'92t happen. It didn\'92t happen at a wedding.\'93\
Walter had come down off the step to get closer to the beer he was not going to be given, which put him at Kathy\'92s elbow, which put him within a metre and a half of the Great Teacher when the Great Teacher arrived to see what everyone was looking at.\
\
\'93Yeah, g\'92day.\'94 Chanta had a beer in each hand and had not worked out how to shake with either. \'93Good speech. Bit sad in the middle.\'94\
\'93It was intended to be.\'94\
\'93Nailed it, then.\'94\
\
Kathy looked at Betty Lee for slightly too long.\
\'93What?\'94 said Betty Lee.\
\'93How old are you?\'94\
\'93Rude,\'94 said Betty Lee.\
\'93She\'92s terrific, isn\'92t she,\'94 said Chanta, pleased. \'93They\'92re all terrific. It\'92s the clean living. I\'92ve been saying, haven\'92t I been saying? Off the smokes, plenty of fresh air. Marguerita had a crook knee for six years and now she\'92s doing the splits at parties.\'94 He raised one of the beers in tribute. \'93Whole crew\'92s blooming. Honest to tax, they walk in ordinary and about a fortnight later they\'92re all glowing away like they\'92ve swallowed a lamp. I put it down to the catering.\'94\
\'93Fortnight,\'94 said Kathy.\
\'93About a fortnight. Give or take.\'94\
Kathy looked at Hayley. Hayley had stopped writing.\
\'93Chanta,\'94 said Kathy. \'93The Elvines over there by the curtains.\'94\
\'93Dianne and that lot.\'94 Chanta brightened. \'93Yeah, they\'92re lovely.\'94\
\'93How lovely?\'94\
\'93Very lovely.\'94\
\'93Chanta.\'94\
\'93They\'92re 
\f2\i very
\f1\i0  lovely,\'93 said Chanta, with the emphasis of a man being asked to repeat himself for no reason he could see, and then, because he was constitutionally unable to leave a silence alone, and because a corner of him had spent his whole life waiting for somebody in a position of authority to ask him a question he could answer, he filled it. \'94All of them, if you want the truth. Every one of them. I\'92m not proud of it. Well. I\'92m a bit proud of it.\'93\
Nobody said anything.\
It took about two seconds, which in a room of that size is a considerable length of time. Hayley got there first, because it was her job. The High Elvis got there second and did not move a muscle. Kathy got there third and put both hands flat on the bar. Sarah Jacobson, twelve feet away on a throne with no padding, got there somewhere in among that and turned her head very slowly towards the curtains.\
Walter got there fourth, and Walter was fifteen, and Walter was already grinning like a split melon before anyone had said a word.\
Chanta looked around at the faces.\
\'93What,\'94 he said.\
Then, a moment later: \'93What?\'94\
Then he counted something on his fingers, twice, and stopped.\
\'93Oh,\'94 said Chanta.\
\'93Say it out loud,\'94 said Kathy. \'93Somebody say it out loud, because I\'92ve been carrying it round in a bag since Tuesday and I\'92d like to hear how stupid it sounds.\'94\
\'93He\'92s carrying the other thing,\'94 said Hayley. \'93The one you can grow nine of by Christmas.\'94\
\'93He\'92s carrying it, he\'92s been carrying it for months, and he\'92s been distributing it like a man handing out flyers.\'94 Kathy did not look at Chanta while she said it, which was as close to mercy as she got. \'93Eleven Elvines walked into a gas attack designed to kill Elvines and walked out of it. And the only thing eleven Elvines have in common in this entire taxing city is standing there in a cape holding two beers.\'94\
\'93Three, now,\'94 said Chanta, who had acquired another one.\
From the throne, Sarah said: \'93Then it can be moved.\'94\
She said it the way a person says a thing they have just this second thought of. She had put no particular weight on it. She was looking at the curtains and not at anybody.\
\'93Ms Jacobson?\'94 said the High Elvis.\
\'93If it got from him to them, it isn\'92t stuck in him. That\'92s all. Things that move once move again.\'94 She shifted her weight in the throne, which took a while, and which she conducted with the patience of a woman lowering a piano. \'93You don\'92t need a laboratory to spread something that\'92s already spreading. You need to stop treating it like an accident.\'94\
\'93Holy Momma \'97\'94\
\'93Sarah.\'94\
\'93Sarah,\'94 said the High Elvis, \'93you are describing a public health policy.\'94\
\'93I\'92m describing what\'92s already happened. You can call it what you like.\'94\
Kathy had gone very still, and then she started to laugh, and it was not a nice laugh, and she put her hand over her eyes.\
\'93Oh, no,\'94 she said. \'93No, no, no.\'94\
\'93What,\'94 said Hayley.\
\'93I\'92m the doctor. I\'92m going to have to be the one who says it.\'94 Kathy took her hand down. \'93Right. Medical opinion. Everybody has to root everybody.\'94\
There was a pause of the kind that gets described in Parliamentary records as 
\f2\i the House being briefly unable to proceed
\f1\i0 .\
\'93That\'92s the prescription,\'94 said Kathy. \'93That\'92s it. That\'92s the whole of it. It goes body to body, it goes fast, it\'92s already got a head start of about eleven Elvines and however many of Chanta\'92s ladies, and there are what, ninety-odd thousand of you left, and the thing that\'92s killing you works in 
\f2\i hours
\f1\i0 . You can\'92t outrun it in a dish. You can outrun it in a bed.\'93 She spread her hands. \'94I\'92d like the record to show I was joking for the first four seconds.\'93\
\'93You were not joking,\'94 said Hayley.\
\'93I was joking for four seconds.\'94\
\
Tom Heyward.\
\
\'93I\'92ll do it, and I\'92ll do it in the street, and I\'92ll do it in front of the Health Department.\'94 She was not shouting. It was worse than shouting. \'93Do you know what I\'92ve been doing for eight days, Tom? I\'92ve been going to funerals in perfect health. I\'92ve been the healthiest thing at four funerals. Cousins of mine are in the ground and I have never felt better in my life and nobody would tell me why.\'94 She turned on Kathy. \'93Is that it? Is that why?\'94\
\
